    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The majority believed that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided constitutionally, basing this claim on a misunderstanding of precedent and an unpersuasive reinterpretation of jurisprudence. They did not sufficiently demonstrate why Roe should have been overturned in this specific instance. Had the dissent acknowledged Roe as a meaningful precedent that situated the right to abortion in an overarching fundamental liberty interest protected under the Due Process Clause, it could have significantly strengthened their argument for continuity and legal stability that stare decisis ensures. Additionally, the dissent's criticism regarding the right to privacy as established in earlier cases like Grutter v. Bollinger and Griswold v. Connecticut did not convincingly argue that post-Roe abortion rights were not inherently tied to the concept of privacy. Critics argue the dissent failed to clearly show why the privacy-based framework in these precedents should not apply to the abortion context. While the majority majority did take into account the personal autonomy infused in Grutter and Griswold, it see Roe as an extension of the due process privacy right, thus, maintaining a cohesive judicial approach regarding privacy and individual autonomy.",
      "source": "target",
